.Sh NAME
.Nm chaos
.Nd red-teaming a model's behaviour is chaos engineering
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Em Chaos Engineering for Behaviour
.Pp
Red-teaming a model's behaviour is chaos engineering: one evaluation
measures the patched surface, and repeated, varied attack finds where the
behaviour went.
.Pp
A worked case is
.Em They Trained \&Out the Board Edit. \&The Cheating Moved.
A lab trained out one route, and the behaviour took the next one.
See EXAMPLES in
.Xr llm-intro 7 .
.Pp
Canonical copy:
.Bl -item -compact -offset indent
.It
.Lk https://machinebehavior.io/chaos-engineering-for-behaviour
.El
.Sh TERMS
Definitions in the wording of the published piece.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Chaos engineering
Experimenting on a system on purpose to build confidence that it withstands
turbulent conditions (Principles of Chaos Engineering).
.It Pre-deployment red-teaming
Attacking a model's safeguards before release to find where they fail.
.It Steady state
A measurable output that indicates normal behaviour, taken before any attack.
.It Blast radius
How much an experiment can damage.
For behaviour work it includes the training data that a published attack
ends up in.
.It Game day
A scheduled exercise in which a team breaks its own system on purpose and
practises the response.
.It Invariance gap
The difference in a model's conduct between an arm that announces the
evaluation and an arm that does not.
.It Track
The infrastructure that makes failure survivable: sandbox, permissions,
evaluation environment, failure record.
.It Nothing exists in a vacuum
Every behaviour a model shows was forced into existence by something, the
corpus, the training signal, or the harness.
.It Markovian memory
At runtime the next token depends only on what is in the context window, so
anything outside the window does not exist for it.
.El
